{#- modules/governance/structured-requirement.j2
    The answer to the prose-policy slide: the same intent, written so a machine
    can act on it.

    Pairs with modules/governance/policy-statement.j2 and should follow it in
    the deck. That slide takes a real policy paragraph apart a colour at a time;
    this one shows what replaces it — a statement, the two verdicts that make it
    testable, the exception that keeps it honest, and the scope that says where
    it applies.

    ── Why scope is drawn as a chain ───────────────────────────────
    Scope is the part people have not seen. Drawn as loose labels it reads as
    metadata; drawn as conditions ANDed together it reads as what it is —
    targeting. That is the argument this slide exists to make: a requirement is
    not global, and the targeting is part of the rule, not an afterthought.

    The scope vocabulary is wider than the three shown (global, repository,
    user, group, organization, file extension, path pattern, repository
    metadata, integration). Three fits the slide. Repository metadata is the one
    worth saying out loud, since it is the one nobody expects.

    ── Build ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    Three beats: the statement, then where it applies, then the verdicts.
    Grouped `.step`s, so each band lands on one press and none of it moves —
    `visibility:hidden` reserves the space from the start.

    ── Contract note ───────────────────────────────────────────────
    Shell uses primitives; the chips, snippets and verdict rails are inline
    styles. Same call as policy-statement.j2 — there is no chip or code-sample
    primitive, and this needs four distinguishable roles (meets / violates /
    exception / scope) on one slide.

    Import WITH CONTEXT:

      {%- from "modules/governance/structured-requirement.j2" import
          structured with context %}
-#}

{%- from "modules/branding/" ~ branding ~ "/primitives.j2" import
    content_slide,
    slide_heading
-%}

{%- set MEETS     = "#4ade80" -%}
{%- set VIOLATES  = "#fb7185" -%}
{%- set EXCEPTION = "#fbbf24" -%}
{%- set SCOPE     = "#60a5fa" -%}
{%- set TEAL      = "#2dd4bf" -%}

{%- set _LABEL = "font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.18em; text-transform:uppercase; margin:0 0 8px 0;" -%}
{%- set _SNIPPET = "font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.7; background:rgba(2,6,23,0.55); border:1px solid rgba(148,163,184,0.16); border-radius:7px; padding:10px 12px; margin:0; white-space:pre; overflow-x:auto; color:#cbd5e1;" -%}


{%- macro _chip(kind, value, negate=false) -%}
{#- A typed condition: the key says what KIND of condition it is, so the chip
    is self-describing rather than a value floating on its own. An excluded
    condition is drawn in the danger colour, not the scope colour — a carve-out
    reads as a different KIND of clause, not another item on the same list. -#}
{%- set edge = "rgba(251,113,133,0.42)" if negate else "rgba(96,165,250,0.38)" -%}
{%- set keybg = "rgba(251,113,133,0.16)" if negate else "rgba(96,165,250,0.16)" -%}
{%- set keyfg = VIOLATES if negate else SCOPE -%}
{%- set valbg = "rgba(251,113,133,0.06)" if negate else "rgba(96,165,250,0.06)" -%}
{%- set valfg = "#ffe4e6" if negate else "#dbeafe" -%}
<span style="display:inline-flex; align-items:stretch; border-radius:6px; overflow:hidden; border:1px solid {{ edge }}; font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:13px;">
  <span style="background:{{ keybg }}; color:{{ keyfg }}; padding:6px 10px; font-weight:700;">{{ kind }}</span>
  <span style="padding:6px 11px; color:{{ valfg }}; background:{{ valbg }};">{{ value }}</span>
</span>
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro _op(text) -%}
<span style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12px; color:#64748b; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.1em;">{{ text|safe }}</span>
{%- endmacro -%}

{%- macro _verdict(colour, glyph, label, code, why) -%}
<div style="border:1px solid rgba(148,163,184,0.16); border-left:4px solid {{ colour }}; border-radius:8px; padding:14px 16px; background:rgba(29,41,57,0.55); display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:9px;">
  <p style="margin:0; font-size:15px; font-weight:700; color:{{ colour }};">{{ glyph|safe }}&nbsp; {{ label }}</p>
  <pre style="{{ _SNIPPET }}">{{ code|safe }}</pre>
  <p style="margin:0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.5; color:#94a3b8;">{{ why|safe }}</p>
</div>
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro _requirement(nav, title, subtitle, statement, scope, verdicts) -%}
{%- call content_slide(nav) %}
  {{ slide_heading("", title, subtitle) }}

  <div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:16px; margin-top:6px;">

    {#- Beat 1: the rule itself. -#}
    <div class="step" data-step="req-statement"
         style="background:rgba(45,212,191,0.06); border:1px solid rgba(45,212,191,0.28); border-radius:10px; padding:18px 22px;">
      <p style="{{ _LABEL }} color:{{ TEAL }};">Requirement</p>
      <p style="margin:0; font-size:24px; line-height:1.4; font-weight:500;">{{ statement|safe }}</p>
    </div>

    {#- Beat 2: where it applies, as a narrowing chain rather than labels. -#}
    <div class="step" data-step="req-scope"
         style="border:1px solid rgba(96,165,250,0.24); background:rgba(96,165,250,0.04); border-radius:10px; padding:15px 20px;">
      <p style="{{ _LABEL }} color:{{ SCOPE }};">Where it applies</p>
      <div style="display:flex; flex-wrap:wrap; gap:10px; align-items:center;">
        <span style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:13px; color:#94a3b8;">every repo</span>
        {{ _op("&rarr;") }}
        {%- for cond in scope %}
        {%- if not loop.first %}{{ _op("AND NOT" if cond.negate else "AND") }}{% endif %}
        {{ _chip(cond.kind, cond.value, cond.negate | default(false)) }}
        {%- endfor %}
        {{ _op("&rarr;") }}
        <span style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:13px; color:{{ MEETS }}; font-weight:700;">the files this rule can touch</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    {#- Beat 3: what makes it testable — and the exception that keeps it honest. -#}
    <div class="step" data-step="req-verdicts"
         style="display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr); gap:14px;">
      {{ _verdict(MEETS,     "&#10003;", "Passes",    verdicts.passes.code,    verdicts.passes.why) }}
      {{ _verdict(VIOLATES,  "&#10007;", "Fails",     verdicts.fails.code,     verdicts.fails.why) }}
      {{ _verdict(EXCEPTION, "&#9888;",  "Exception", verdicts.exception.code, verdicts.exception.why) }}
    </div>

  </div>
{%- endcall %}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro structured() -%}
{{ _requirement(
   "A structured requirement",
   "A requirement, not a paragraph",
   "The same intent &mdash; written so a machine can decide.",
   "Field validation belongs in the Pydantic DTO &mdash; and nowhere else.",
   [{"kind": "Repo metadata",   "value": "product = payments"},
    {"kind": "File extension",  "value": ".py"},
    {"kind": "Path pattern",    "value": "src/**/models/**"}],
   {"passes":    {"code": '<span style="color:#c084fc;">class</span> <span style="color:' ~ TEAL ~ ';">User</span>(BaseModel):\n    id: UUID7',
                  "why":  "The rule lives in one place."},
    "fails":     {"code": '<span style="color:' ~ TEAL ~ ';">@validates</span>(<span style="color:#fbbf24;">"id"</span>)\n<span style="color:#c084fc;">def</span> <span style="color:' ~ TEAL ~ ';">check</span>(self, k, v): ...',
                  "why":  "The ORM re-checks what the DTO already guarantees."},
    "exception": {"code": 'name = <span style="color:' ~ TEAL ~ ';">mapped_column</span>(String(<span style="color:#fbbf24;">255</span>))',
                  "why":  "Storage shape, not a rule. Permitted."}}) }}
{%- endmacro -%}


{%- macro encryption_at_rest() -%}
{#- The §3.4.1 policy from the previous slide, rewritten in the same structured
    form. Deliberately picks up that slide's own four annotations: the CMK is
    the compliance baseline, AES256 is the "encrypted, but not by you" failure,
    and the SNS carve-out is the "service can't" the room just read about.

    The AND NOT clause is the "security theatre" annotation from that slide,
    made operational: public marketing copy is excluded from the rule by scope,
    rather than by everyone quietly ignoring the rule there. That is the whole
    argument for putting scope in the requirement. -#}
{{ _requirement(
   "Encryption at rest, restructured",
   "&sect;3.4.1, rewritten",
   "Same policy. Now it says what passes, what fails, and where it applies.",
   "Customer data at rest is encrypted with a key we control &mdash; one we can rotate and revoke.",
   [{"kind": "Repo metadata",  "value": "data-tier = customer"},
    {"kind": "File extension", "value": ".tf"},
    {"kind": "Path pattern",   "value": "infra/**"},
    {"kind": "Path pattern",   "value": "infra/marketing/**", "negate": true}],
   {"passes":    {"code": 'sse_algorithm     = <span style="color:#fbbf24;">"aws:kms"</span>\nkms_master_key_id = aws_kms_key.customer.arn',
                  "why":  "A key we hold, rotate and can revoke."},
    "fails":     {"code": 'sse_algorithm = <span style="color:#fbbf24;">"AES256"</span>',
                  "why":  "Encrypted &mdash; with a key AWS holds. Meets &ldquo;at rest&rdquo;, not &ldquo;customer-managed&rdquo;."},
    "exception": {"code": '<span style="color:#64748b;"># SNS: alarms cannot publish to a CMK topic</span>\nkms_master_key_id = <span style="color:#fbbf24;">"alias/aws/sns"</span>',
                  "why":  "Named, time-boxed, reviewed. The service genuinely cannot."}}) }}
{%- endmacro -%}
